{
  "concept": "The moment of inertia of a uniform disk is built by summing thin concentric rings, each of which carries all of its mass dm at a single distance r from the rotation axis, so its contribution is r² dm.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: face-on view of the disk, looking down the rotation axis",
    "Left panel: large blue-to-white radially shaded filled circle representing the uniform disk of radius R, thin blue-grey outline",
    "Left panel: vertical axis arrow labeled 'x' pointing up, horizontal axis arrow labeled 'y' pointing right, both drawn through the disk center",
    "Left panel: 'z' label at the center marking the axis emerging out of the page",
    "Left panel: orange annulus (ring) concentric with the rim, drawn at radius r with visible width dr",
    "Left panel: double-headed black arrow from center to outer rim labeled 'R' (drawn toward lower-left)",
    "Left panel: arrow from center to the orange ring labeled 'r' (drawn toward lower-right)",
    "Left panel: short arrow with label 'dr' pointing at the radial width of the orange ring (upper-right)",
    "Right panel: oblique perspective view of the same disk drawn as a shaded ellipse with an orange rim",
    "Right panel: vertical axis arrow labeled 'z' through the center, horizontal axis arrow labeled 'y' to the right, oblique axis arrow labeled 'x' toward the lower left",
    "Right panel: curved arrow looping around the z axis indicating the sense of rotation",
    "Right panel: arrow from center out to a point on the ring labeled 'r'",
    "Right panel: small marker on the ring labeled 'dm' (the mass element)",
    "Right panel: orange elliptical annulus matching the left panel's ring radius and width",
    "Readout panel: current r/R, dr/R, ring area dA = 2πr dr, ring mass dm = (M/πR²)·dA, and contribution r² dm",
    "Accumulation bar / running-total indicator for ΣI = Σ r² dm approaching ½MR²",
    "Legend: orange = highlighted differential ring, blue = rest of the disk"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "ringRadius",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Ring radius r / R",
      "range": [0.05, 1.0, 0.01],
      "default": 0.55,
      "teaches": "Moving the same ring outward increases its contribution r² dm sharply, because both the ring's circumference (its mass) and the lever-arm squared grow with r."
    },
    {
      "id": "ringWidth",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Ring width dr / R",
      "range": [0.01, 0.25, 0.01],
      "default": 0.06,
      "teaches": "The ring is a differential element: as dr shrinks, its mass dm shrinks proportionally and every point in it sits at essentially the same radius r, which is what makes r² dm exact."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Face-on (x–y)", "Perspective (tilted)", "Both"],
      "default": "Both",
      "teaches": "The face-on circle and the tilted ellipse are the same object: the integration element is a complete ring encircling the rotation axis, not a point or a radial strip."
    },
    {
      "id": "integrateRings",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Sum rings from center to rim",
      "states": ["idle", "integrating", "done"],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "Stacking every ring from r = 0 to r = R fills the whole disk and the accumulated Σ r² dm converges to I = ½MR², with most of the total arriving from the outer rings."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The whole disk",
      "narration": "A uniform disk of mass M and radius R spins about the z axis through its center. Seen face-on the axis points out of the page; seen in perspective the curved arrow shows the sense of rotation.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Both", "ringRadius": 0.55, "ringWidth": 0.06, "integrateRings": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pick one thin ring",
      "narration": "Highlight a single thin annulus at radius r with width dr. In the perspective view the same ring carries the mass element dm, and every bit of that mass sits at the same distance r from the axis.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Both", "ringRadius": 0.55, "ringWidth": 0.06 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Make the ring differential",
      "narration": "Shrink dr. The ring's area 2πr dr and its mass dm shrink with it, and the spread of radii inside the ring vanishes — this is why dI = r² dm is exact for a ring, not an approximation.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Face-on (x–y)", "ringRadius": 0.55, "ringWidth": 0.02 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide the ring inward",
      "narration": "Near the center the ring is short, holds little mass, and its lever arm is tiny, so its r² dm contribution is almost nothing.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Face-on (x–y)", "ringRadius": 0.12, "ringWidth": 0.02 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide the ring outward",
      "narration": "Push the same-width ring out to the rim: it is now long, holds far more mass, and each gram sits at the largest possible radius, so the contribution jumps dramatically.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Perspective (tilted)", "ringRadius": 0.95, "ringWidth": 0.02 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sum all the rings",
      "narration": "Add rings from the center out to R. They tile the entire disk, and the running total Σ r² dm climbs steeply only in the last part of the sweep, converging on I = ½MR².",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Both", "ringRadius": 1.0, "ringWidth": 0.02, "integrateRings": "done" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A ring is the natural mass element for a disk because all of its mass lies at one radius — and since that mass grows like r while the lever arm counts as r², the outer rings dominate the moment of inertia.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two side-by-side panels on a white background. Left: a large circle with a soft blue-to-white radial/linear gradient fill and thin grey-blue outline, centered on crossed thin black axis arrows — 'x' up, 'y' right, italic 'z' labeled at the center. A concentric orange ring (stroke ~orange/salmon, fill lightly tinted) sits inside it; a double-headed black arrow from center to rim toward the lower-left labeled 'R'; a single arrow from center to the orange ring toward the lower-right labeled 'r'; a short arrow in the upper-right pointing inward at the ring's thickness labeled 'dr'. Right: the same disk drawn as a wide, flattened ellipse (oblique view) with the same blue gradient fill and an orange rim; a vertical 'z' arrow through the center, a 'y' arrow to the right, an 'x' arrow going down-left out of the plane; a small curved arrow circling the top of the z axis for rotation sense; a straight arrow from center to a point on the ring labeled 'r', with a small dot on the ring labeled 'dm'. All labels italic serif, matching the textbook style. Highlighted annulus in both panels must stay synchronized to the r and dr sliders. Below or beside the figure place a compact readout: r/R, dr/R, dA = 2πr dr, dm, and a horizontal bar whose length tracks r² dm, plus a second bar for the accumulated Σ r² dm scaled against ½MR². When the integrate button runs, previously summed rings stay drawn in faded orange so the learner sees the disk filling in from the center outward."
}

